\begin{quote}
Figure 6: Bar charts showing memory efficiency and main-thread
responsiveness under increasing workload

Figure 6 explains the runtime efficiency of the implementation. The
memory-overhead graph shows that HVEOS grows more gradually as network
load increases, while the legacy pipeline consumes substantially more
memory at the same load levels. This in-dicates that modular execution
and selective state handling reduce pressure on system memory. The
thread-blocking comparison further shows that thread-pool offloading
keeps the main execution thread responsive even when graph serialization
workload increases. For example, at the highest tested workload,
synchronous GUI processing blocks the main thread for 850 ms, while
thread-pool offloading limits blocking to 25 ms. This supports the
design decision to offload expensive graph and visualization tasks so
that WebSocket updates and frontend interaction remain responsive.
\end{quote}
\end{minipage} \\
